{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_dfwdoi7s7uttdyaeeswqfmzgbnawrbuowtud3h6zuq24vqcofojq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_011NiPFU4NzcqMLaEA4FHu6Y","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-jsRX3G/translation/src/lib.rs","old_string":"// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Raw memory access\n//\n// All loads/stores through caller-supplied pointers go through\n// `read_volatile`/`write_volatile` rather than `*p` / `*p = v`.\n//\n// This is required for behavioural fidelity, not just for style: a plain Rust\n// deref of a raw pointer emits a language-level null check whenever the crate is\n// compiled with `-C debug-assertions` (the default for the `dev` profile), which\n// turns C's plain faulting load into a Rust panic — i.e. `SIGABRT` instead of\n// the `SIGSEGV` the C library produces for `process_string(NULL)`,\n// `init_matrix(NULL)`, `arity(2, NULL)`, ... The volatile accessors compile to\n// the same bare `mov` gcc emits at `-O0`, so the fault (and the tolerance of\n// misaligned pointers on x86) matches the C library in every cargo profile.\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------","new_string":"// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Raw memory access\n//\n// Every load/store through a caller-supplied pointer goes through `load`/`store`\n// below instead of `*p` / `*p = v`. This is required for behavioural fidelity,\n// not style — the C library accesses memory with plain `mov` instructions that\n// check nothing, and the obvious Rust spellings all add a check that changes the\n// observable behaviour as soon as the crate is built with `-C debug-assertions`\n// (the default for the `dev` profile):\n//\n//   * `*p` / `*p = v`                    -> panics on a NULL pointer, so\n//                                           `process_string(NULL)` would abort\n//                                           (`SIGABRT`) where C faults\n//                                           (`SIGSEGV`).\n//   * `ptr::read_volatile`               -> no NULL check, but panics on a\n//                                           *misaligned* pointer, which C reads\n//                                           happily on x86-64.\n//   * `ptr::read_unaligned`              -> no alignment check, but panics on\n//                                           NULL.\n//\n// Doing the volatile access through an `align_of == 1` newtype satisfies both:\n// there is no NULL check and no alignment check, and it still compiles to the\n// single `mov (%rdi),%eax` that gcc emits. NULL therefore faults with `SIGSEGV`\n// exactly like the C library, and misaligned buffers are read/written exactly\n// like the C library, in every cargo profile.\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n/// A `T` with no alignment requirement.\n#[repr(C, packed)]\n#[derive(Clone, Copy)]\nstruct Unaligned<T: Copy>(T);\n\n/// Load a `T`, checking nothing: the C equivalent of `mov (%p), %reg`.\n#[inline(always)]\nunsafe fn load<T: Copy>(p: *const T) -> T {\n    unsafe { read_volatile(p as *const Unaligned<T>).0 }\n}\n\n/// Store a `T`, checking nothing: the C equivalent of `mov %reg, (%p)`.\n#[inline(always)]\nunsafe fn store<T: Copy>(p: *mut T, v: T) {\n    unsafe { write_volatile(p as *mut Unaligned<T>, Unaligned(v)) }\n}"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2611,"cache_read_input_tokens":193943,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2611,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fbb3b3de-b324-47ff-ab96-c06ce4d749a0","uuid":"46695982-d24d-4606-bfe2-eca3d282538b","timestamp":"2026-08-27T