{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_xgg5daaiyb22ri5aaheieup5w5aljctelbimkjsyjvsgm45jnawq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01Tg1L69WgtcnQ3vQarynmgK","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-jsRX3G/translation/src/lib.rs","old_string":"    // `black_box` hides the provenance of the two allocations from LLVM.\n    //\n    // Without it, LLVM knows these pointers come from `malloc` (hence `noalias`)\n    // and that the memory is `free`d again, so at `opt-level > 0` it deletes the\n    // two stores below *and* the later load, and answers the `*uninit_ptr > 0`\n    // test from `val1` in a register. gcc at `-O0` really does store and reload,\n    // so the two implementations then disagree whenever the two allocations\n    // happen to alias: the C library reads back `val2` (the value written last)\n    // while an optimised Rust build would report `val1`. That case is reachable\n    // through the FFI boundary with an interposed allocator, and it is exactly\n    // what `tests/phase_c_errors.rs::e24_pointer_order_branches` exercises.","new_string":"    // The `store`/`load` accessors used below are what make this function\n    // faithful at `opt-level > 0`: with plain `*ptr1 = val1` / `*uninit_ptr`,\n    // LLVM knows the pointers come from `malloc` (hence `noalias`) and that the\n    // memory is `free`d again, so it deletes both stores *and* the later load and\n    // answers the `*uninit_ptr > 0` test from `val1` in a register. gcc at `-O0`\n    // really does store and reload, so the two implementations would then\n    // disagree whenever the two allocations alias: the C library reads back\n    // `val2` (the value written last) while an optimised Rust build would report\n    // `val1`. That case is reachable through the FFI boundary with an interposed\n    // allocator and is exercised by\n    // `tests/phase_c_error