{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_mxetpaki5qsyyoe6llobxmw5fxmkmkje7bee53qp2w4s37mhopxa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases passed, and the process found **three real divergences** in the Rust translation.\n\n## What the C library is\n`c_src/src/lib.c` — 9 exported functions: `shift_array`, `process_string`, `apply_bitmask`, `init_matrix`, `compare_allocations`, `arity4`, `arity3`, `arity2`, `arity`.\n\n## Bugs found in the Rust translation (all fixed)\n\n1. **`SIGABRT` instead of C's `SIGSEGV` on NULL** — the C code dereferences unchecked pointers (`process_string(NULL)`, `arity(2, NULL)`, `init_matrix(NULL)`). Rust's plain `*p` emits a language-level null check under `debug-assertions`, so it aborted with a panic message instead of faulting. Found by `e7_crash_parity` (compares terminating signals across child processes).\n\n2. **Abort on misaligned pointers** — my first fix used `read_volatile`, which has no null check but *does* carry an alignment check. C reads `params[i]` with a plain `mov` that tolerates any address. Fixed with a `#[repr(C, packed)]` newtype (no null *and* no alignment check); verified it compiles to exactly `mov (%rdi),%eax`, the same instruction gcc emits.\n\n3. **Optimized builds deleted memory traffic C actually observes** — in `compare_allocations`, LLVM knows both pointers are `malloc`'d (`noalias`) and freed, so at `opt-level>0` it removed both stores and the reload and answered `*uninit_ptr > 0` from `val1` in a register. gcc at `-O0` stores and reloads, so when the two allocations alias, C reports `val2` and Rust reported `val1`. Found only in the release profile, via an interposed `malloc`.\n\n## Two harness bugs I found and fixed (these would have given false results)\n\n- **A flaky comparison, failing ~1 run in 5.** `compare_allocations` compares two `malloc` addresses, so its value is a function of the *shared* allocator state — `probe_alloc.rs` proves this by loading the same C `.so` twice and showing the two C instances diverging from each other identically. I replaced the parity assumption with `normalize_allocator()`, which canonicalizes the tcache bin to force `ptr1 < ptr2` or `ptr1 > ptr2`. That made comparisons deterministic *and* stronger (exact expected values asserted, both branches hit deliberately). 0 failures in 15 further full-suite runs.\n- A race compiling the `LD_PRELOAD` fixture, and child-output parsing (libtest prints `test name ... ` without a newline).\n\n## Coverage and evidence\n\n| gate | result |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` | symbol diff **empty** (9/9), `ldd -r` clean, in every configuration |\n| `CONFIGS.md` | **54/54** rows pass, 400 seeded-random inputs per row |\n| `ERRORS.md` | **39/39** rows pass, incl. allocation failure (`LD_PRELOAD`), crash parity, out-of-range enum values, misalignment |\n| configurations | 3 feature selections × 2 profiles + cross-profile `.so` loading — `ALL CONFIGURATIONS PASSED` |\n| test power (`mutation_check.sh`) | dev: 34 caught / **0 blind spots**; release: 32 caught / **0 blind spots** |\n\nThe mutation script classifies its 4-6 survivors as provably behavior-preserving with reasons (e.g. signed-vs-unsigned pointer compare is unobservable because no x86-64 user address has bit 63 set), so \"survived\" is never conflated with \"untested\". `c_src/` is unmodified apart from the `build/` directory the task asked me to create."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":561,"cache_read_input_tokens":277549,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":561,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fbb3b3de-b324-47ff-ab96-c06ce4d749a0","uuid":"0c57e4d6-b9bf-471e-91bf-88a988d6350d","timestamp":"2026-08-27T22:54:43.045Z"}
